% M7-T9. `indomain_split` bisecting a domain WITH AN INTERIOR HOLE, and the split
% point landing exactly ON the hole -- the case a naive midpoint gets wrong and the
% one M1-T55's bridges exist for.
%
% int_ne(x, 2) leaves x with lo = 1, hi = 4 and a hole at 2. The RANGE midpoint is
% 1 + (4 - 1) / 2 = 2, which is the hole; the VALUE median of {1, 3, 4} is 3, which
% is what `indomain_median` would want and what this must NOT do. Pushing x <= 2
% makes Domain.settle walk the bound past the hole to x <= 1, so the decision lands
% strictly STRONGER than the order literal its nogood negates -- the exact shape
% test_hole_split_sweep in test/unit/test_engine.ml sweeps.
%
% The int_lin_ne rows forbid every reachable value of x + y, so the model is UNSAT
% and the search must explore BOTH sides of the hole split before it can say so.
% int_lin_ne is value-consistent, so none of them prunes at the root: the refutation
% is a real tree, not a root conflict.
var 1..4: x :: output_var;
var 1..4: y :: output_var;
constraint int_ne(x, 2);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 2);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 3);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 4);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 5);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 6);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 7);
constraint int_lin_ne([1, 1], [x, y], 8);
solve :: int_search([x, y], input_order, indomain_split, complete) satisfy;
